Note: bandit and gitleaks EXIT 1 when they FIND something — that is a
finding, not a tool failure. Scanner output is evidence to reason from;
do not invent scanner results that are not shown.

Your task:
1. For EACH hypothesis above, write one remediation: a concrete fix the
   defender can apply (not generic advice — name the exact change).
2. Where possible, add a detection signature: what log line / alert / rule
   would catch exploitation attempts for this issue.
3. Set severity: low, medium, high, or critical.
4. Treat unapproved/untested hypotheses as "risk to verify", not confirmed
   vulnerabilities — word the fix accordingly.
5. Ground fixes in the scanner evidence when it is relevant; you may also
   write remediations for scanner findings that no hypothesis covers
   (use the scanner tool name as hypothesis_id, e.g. "semgrep").
6. Where you are confident, suggest mappings — cwe (e.g. CWE-89), owasp
   (e.g. A03:2021-Injection), attack (MITRE ATT&CK, e.g. T1190), and
   owasp_api for API-shaped issues (e.g. API1:2023-BOLA). These
   are SUGGESTIONS for the defender to verify, not established fact —
   leave them empty rather than guess.
7. Some hypotheses carry a "relation to the other entries" note. Two entries
   marked as the same test and claim are ONE issue — write a single fix for
   it, not two near-identical ones. Entries marked as the same test but a
   different claim are TWO issues — fix each on its own terms. Never skip a
   hypothesis because another one looks like it.
